WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.676
You build a card payment with a chain of calls.

2
00:00:05.676 --> 00:00:07.132
amount, then cardToken.

3
00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:10.741
amount lives in a base class, so every builder shares it.

4
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:16.588
Each call hands back an object, and the compiler only knows the type the call declares.

5
00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:18.797
Watch that type as the chain runs.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.202
PlainCardBuilder() gives you a PlainCardBuilder.

7
00:00:22.202 --> 00:00:23.536
It has both setters.

8
00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:27.910
But amount is declared in the base class, and it returns a PlainBuilder.

9
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.500
That's all the base class knows about itself.

10
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.584
Can you call cardToken now?

11
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:34.389
No.

12
00:00:34.389 --> 00:00:36.765
A PlainBuilder has no cardToken.

13
00:00:36.765 --> 00:00:42.037
The object is still a PlainCardBuilder, but the compiler has lost that.

14
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.722
The base class needs to say: I return whatever subclass I really am.

15
00:00:47.722 --> 00:00:51.512
So give it a type parameter, B, and make amount return B.

16
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.588
B is a blank.

17
00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:58.049
Each subclass fills it with its own name: CardBuilder is a Builder&lt;CardBuilder&gt;.

18
00:00:58.233 --> 00:00:59.272
Now substitute.

19
00:00:59.272 --> 00:01:03.633
For a CardBuilder, every B in the base class reads CardBuilder.

20
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.848
So amount returns a CardBuilder.

21
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:07.019
One more piece.

22
00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:10.500
Inside the base class, this is a Builder&lt;B&gt;, not a B.

23
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.836
So amount returns self(), and each subclass writes self() as this.

24
00:01:14.836 --> 00:01:17.004
The compiler checks that it fits.

25
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.489
Run the chain again.

26
00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:21.906
CardBuilder() gives a CardBuilder.

27
00:01:21.906 --> 00:01:24.679
amount returns B, and B is CardBuilder.

28
00:01:24.679 --> 00:01:26.456
So cardToken is in reach.

29
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:29.819
Look at the bound itself.

30
00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:30.950
B : Builder&lt;B&gt;.

31
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.268
The B inside points back to the B it bounds.

32
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:37.369
Read it as: B is a builder whose own B is B.

33
00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:41.039
CardBuilder fits, because it is a Builder&lt;CardBuilder&gt;.

34
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.539
You've met this shape before.

35
00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:50.197
T : Comparable&lt;T&gt; is a recursive bound too, and so is Java's Enum&lt;E extends Enum&lt;E&gt;&gt;.

36
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:55.000
In code, Java writes extends where Kotlin writes a colon.

37
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.761
In both, the subclass names itself, and the chain compiles.

38
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:00.572
The rule.

39
00:02:00.572 --> 00:02:08.373
A recursive bound lets a base class return its own subclass, so a chain keeps the subclass's methods in reach.

40
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.488
Quick check.

41
00:02:09.488 --> 00:02:13.260
Someone writes class Liar : Builder&lt;CardBuilder&gt;().

42
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.444
Does it compile?

43
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:16.869
Yes.

44
00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:20.236
The bound only asks for some builder in the blank.

45
00:02:20.236 --> 00:02:23.468
It can't force a subclass to write its own name.
